.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1238 PM PDT Fri Sep 25 2026
(This evening through Sunday night)

Lingering stratus is dissipating across the East Bay, the San Mateo
coast, the southern Monterey Bay and the Big Sur Coast, while high
clouds flow over the North Bay. A weak and dry cold front passed
through the SF Bay Area this morning and is making its way through
the Central Coast, bringing breezy conditions in its wake through
the afternoon and evening hours, and starting a cooling and
moistening trend across the region. High temperatures today will
range from the upper 70s to middle 80s in the inland valleys, the
upper 80s to lower 90s in the southern reaches of Monterey County,
the upper 60s to the upper 70s near the Bays, and the lower to
middle 60s along the Pacific coast. Overnight tonight, some weak
offshore flow is possible in the higher elevations of the North
and East Bay.

By Saturday, a trough will develop in the eastern Pacific, and
through the weekend, strengthen into a cut-off low and march into
the state, continuing the cooling trend towards the rest of the
weekend. Highs in the inland valleys will range from the 70s into
the lower 80s, while the coast and the Bays see highs ranging
from the 60s to the lower 70s. The warmest spots in southern
Monterey County will rise to the upper 80s to the lower 90s on
Saturday. Nocturnal drying is possible Saturday night into Sunday
morning within higher elevations above the marine layer.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1238 PM PDT Fri Sep 25 2026
(Monday through next Thursday)

Ridging, or at the very least more zonal flow, will return to the
region on Monday as the cut-off low moves off to the southeast,
causing temperatures to rise near to slightly above (up to 10
degrees) the seasonal average for the middle of next week. High
temperatures in the middle 80s to lower 90s are expected in the
inland valleys, the middle 70s to the middle 80s near the bays, and
the middle 60s to lower 70s along the coast. Beyond that, forecast
uncertainty rises as ensemble model cluster analysis starts to
diverge on the upper level pattern across the western United States.

